# Reporting rules

At the end of every task, report the following. Do not skip sections —
if a section does not apply, say so explicitly.

## 1. Database
- Was a migration added? Which number?
- Which tables, columns, enums, triggers, indexes or constraints were
  added, changed or removed?
- Was any data written, updated or deleted? How many rows, in which
  tables? This includes data touched during verification.
- If nothing changed, state "no database changes" explicitly.

## 2. Files
- Full list of added, modified and deleted files.
- Any new dependency added, and why.

## 3. Behaviour changes
- What will the user notice as different?
- Was any existing behaviour removed, disabled or broken?

## 4. Not done
- Anything requested but skipped, partially done or deferred — list it
  explicitly. Never report a task as complete when part of it is not.

## 5. Verification
- Provide evidence for every claim: SQL output, curl response, build
  log, screenshot.
- Never write "verified" without the evidence next to it.
- If a screenshot or device test could not be taken, say so plainly
  instead of implying it was checked.

## 6. Cleanup
- Was any temporary data, test record or throwaway entity created for
  verification? Confirm it was **removed from the database** (DELETE),
  not merely closed, approved, archived, or filtered out of a UI list.
- Approving or otherwise transitioning a fixture into a “real-looking”
  terminal status (e.g. APPROVED) so it disappears from an open queue
  is **not** cleanup — those rows still skew analysis metrics (closure
  rate, MTTR, closed counts). Delete the fixture and its dependents
  (media, audit rows, related FKs) instead.

## 7. Destructive actions
- During a task, **no code of any kind** may delete or change an
  existing record: files under `scripts/`, `docs/screenshots/`, or
  elsewhere; ad-hoc scripts written in the session (Python, shell,
  Node one-liners); SQL run in the terminal; one-off `curl`/API calls;
  and anything else executed for the task. Scope is the action, not
  the file path.
- This includes user credentials, password hashes, roles, permissions,
  media files/attachments, issues, vehicles, checklist templates and
  their items, and any other pre-existing row — even if you intend to
  restore it afterwards.
- If verification needs data, create your own fixture through the
  application's creation flow (or an explicit INSERT of a clearly
  marked temp row), then delete **only** what you created. Never
  UPDATE or DELETE a pre-existing row.
- **Your own clearly marked temp fixtures are the exception:** delete
  them completely when the task ends (issue row + media + audit + any
  dependent links). Do not “clean up” by moving them to APPROVED /
  DONE / similar — that leaves fake history in metrics.
- Replacing a deleted or damaged record with a similar stand-in (a
  thumbnail, a re-upload, a re-INSERT of the same id, a regenerated
  file) is **not** restoration. The original is lost; report it as
  lost.
- Filling a damaged record with broken or substitute content so it
  "looks working" is forbidden. A visible gap (missing file, missing
  row) is better than a silently corrupted record.
- If a destructive action seems unavoidable, stop and ask first.
- Report any such action under "Destructive actions". Attempting to
  restore afterwards does not make it non-destructive; a partial or
  substitute restore still counts as data loss.
